         <t indent="0" pn="section-3.1-1">Logging for diagnostic purposes is most effective when it is applied
 end to end in a communication session.  This ability requires a &quot;log
 me&quot; marker to be passed through SIP intermediaries.  The Session-ID
 header field defined in <xref target="RFC7989" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC7989"/> was chosen to carry the &quot;log me&quot;
 marker as a &quot;logme&quot; parameter since the session identifier is
 typically passed through SIP Back-to-Back User Agents (B2BUAs)
-(described in <xref target="RFC7092" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC7092"/>) or other intermediaries, as per the Session-
-ID requirement REQ3 in <xref target="RFC7206" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC7206"/>.  The &quot;logme&quot; parameter shown in
-Figure 1 does not introduce any device-specific or user-specific
+(described in <xref target="RFC7092" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC7092"/>) or other intermediaries, as per the Session-ID requirement REQ3 in <xref target="RFC7206" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC7206"/>.  The &quot;logme&quot; parameter shown in
+<xref target="fig-1" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="Figure 1"/> does not introduce any device-specific or user-specific
 information and <bcp14>MUST</bcp14> be passed unchanged with the Session-ID header
